### What are the three delivery semantics?

Delivery semantics describe the guarantee a messaging system makes about how many times a message is delivered and processed in the presence of failures. The semantic you get depends on how you configure producer acknowledgments and retries, and when the consumer commits its offset relative to processing.

```java
package com.bank.semantics;

public class DeliverySemanticsOverview {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Delivery Semantics:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("At-most-once:  0 or 1 delivery  (may LOSE)");
        System.out.println("At-least-once: 1 or more        (may DUPLICATE)");
        System.out.println("Exactly-once:  exactly 1         (neither)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Banking choice by data type:");
        System.out.println("  Metrics:   at-most-once (loss OK)");
        System.out.println("  Transfers: at-least-once + idempotency");
        System.out.println("  Kafka-to-Kafka pipelines: exactly-once");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
At-most-once: may lose, never duplicate
At-least-once: may duplicate, never lose
Exactly-once: neither loss nor duplication
```

### What causes at-most-once delivery?

At-most-once happens when you commit or acknowledge before safely processing. On the consumer side, if a consumer commits its offset immediately after receiving a record but before processing it, and then crashes, the record is marked as done and is never reprocessed. On the producer side, `acks=0` means the producer fires and forgets without waiting for the broker.

```java
package com.bank.semantics;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;

// DANGEROUS for banking: at-most-once consumer
public class AtMostOnceConsumer {

    public void processRecord(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record) {
        // Step 1: Commit offset FIRST (at-most-once)
        // consumer.commitSync();
        System.out.println("Offset committed: " + record.offset());

        // Step 2: Process the record
        // If crash happens HERE, record is lost (offset already committed)
        System.out.println("Processing transfer: " + record.value());
        // Transfer never applied, but Kafka thinks it is done
    }
}
```

```text
At-most-once flow:
1. Receive record (offset 42)
2. Commit offset 42 -> Kafka marks it done
3. CRASH before processing
4. Restart -> resumes from offset 43
5. Record 42 LOST (never processed)
```

### What causes at-least-once delivery?

At-least-once happens when you process first and commit afterward. The consumer processes the record and only then commits the offset. If it crashes after processing but before committing, the record is redelivered after restart and processed again.

```java
package com.bank.semantics;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.kafka.support.Acknowledgment;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class AtLeastOnceConsumer {

    // enable.auto.commit=false, ack-mode=MANUAL
    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfers", groupId = "settlement-group")
    public void processTransfer(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record,
                                Acknowledgment ack) {
        // Step 1: Process the record FIRST
        System.out.println("Processing transfer: " + record.key()
            + " offset=" + record.offset());
        // Apply debit, update ledger...

        // Step 2: Commit offset AFTER processing
        ack.acknowledge();
        System.out.println("Offset committed: " + record.offset());
        // If crash between step 1 and step 2:
        // Record redelivered -> processed AGAIN (duplicate)
    }
}
```

```text
At-least-once flow:
1. Receive record (offset 42)
2. Process transfer (debit applied)
3. CRASH before commit
4. Restart -> resumes from offset 42
5. Record 42 REDELIVERED -> processed AGAIN (duplicate debit)
6. Solution: idempotent consumer
```

### How do you make the consumer idempotent?

An idempotent consumer checks whether a record has already been processed before applying the side effect. The two common techniques are a processed-id check in application code and a unique constraint in the database.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class IdempotentSettlementService {

    private final SettlementRepository repo;

    public IdempotentSettlementService(SettlementRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }

    public void settleTransfer(String transferId, String accountId,
                               BigDecimal amount) {
        // Check 1: Application-level idempotency check
        if (repo.existsByTransferId(transferId)) {
            System.out.println("Duplicate detected, skipping: " + transferId);
            return;
        }

        // Process the transfer
        repo.save(transferId, accountId, amount);
        System.out.println("Settled transfer: " + transferId
            + " account=" + accountId + " amount=" + amount);
        // Check 2: UNIQUE constraint on transfer_id column
        // prevents duplicate even if app check has a race condition
    }
}

interface SettlementRepository {
    boolean existsByTransferId(String transferId);
    void save(String transferId, String accountId, BigDecimal amount);
}
```

```text
First delivery:
Settled transfer: TRF-001 account=SAV2001 amount=50000.00

Redelivery after crash:
Duplicate detected, skipping: TRF-001
-> Rajesh never double-debited
```

### What causes exactly-once delivery?

Exactly-once requires coordinated features. On the producer side, idempotence (`enable.idempotence=true`) makes the broker detect and discard duplicate sends. On top of that, Kafka transactions let a producer write to multiple partitions and commit the consumer offsets atomically.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import java.util.Properties;

public class ExactlyOnceProducerConfig {

    public static Properties createConfig() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");
        props.put("key.serializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put("value.serializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");

        // Exactly-once producer settings
        props.put("enable.idempotence", "true");
        props.put("acks", "all");
        props.put("transactional.id", "transfer-enrichment-tx");

        System.out.println("Exactly-once producer configured:");
        System.out.println("  enable.idempotence=true");
        System.out.println("  acks=all");
        System.out.println("  transactional.id=transfer-enrichment-tx");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        createConfig();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Exactly-once producer: idempotence + acks=all + transactional.id
Broker deduplicates retries, transactions make writes atomic.
```

### How is at-least-once different from exactly-once? (comparison table)

| Feature | At-Least-Once | Exactly-Once |
|---|---|---|
| Message loss | Never | Never |
| Duplicates | Possible (consumer handles) | Never (Kafka handles) |
| Offset commit | Manual, after processing | Atomic with writes (transaction) |
| Producer config | acks=all, retries | acks=all, idempotence, transactional.id |
| Consumer config | enable.auto.commit=false | isolation.level=read_committed |
| Side effects | Need app-level idempotency | Kafka-to-Kafka: native; external: idempotent |
| Throughput | Higher | Lower (transaction overhead) |
| Complexity | Simple + idempotent consumer | More complex (transactions) |

```java
package com.bank.comparison;

public class AtLeastOnceVsExactlyOnce {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("At-Least-Once:");
        System.out.println("  Process -> commit -> done");
        System.out.println("  Crash between process and commit -> redelivery");
        System.out.println("  Consumer must be idempotent");
        System.out.println("  Higher throughput, simpler setup");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Exactly-Once:");
        System.out.println("  Begin transaction -> process -> commit offset + writes");
        System.out.println("  Crash -> transaction aborted -> no partial writes");
        System.out.println("  No app-level dedup needed (for Kafka-to-Kafka)");
        System.out.println("  Lower throughput, more complex setup");
    }
}
```

```text
At-least-once: simple + idempotent consumer (banking default)
Exactly-once: transactions (Kafka-to-Kafka pipelines)
```

### What are common mistakes with delivery semantics?

The most common mistake is assuming at-least-once means no duplicates. It means no loss, but duplicates will happen during rebalances and crashes. Another mistake is using exactly-once transactions for external database writes, where they do not help.

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

// WRONG: Assuming at-least-once prevents duplicates.
public class WrongAssumption {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: at-least-once = no duplicates");
        System.out.println("  Reality: crash after processing, before commit");
        System.out.println("  -> record redelivered -> processed AGAIN");
        System.out.println("  -> Rajesh debited TWICE");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: at-least-once + idempotent consumer");
        System.out.println("  -> redelivery detected by transfer id check");
        System.out.println("  -> duplicate skipped -> Rajesh debited ONCE");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: at-least-once without idempotency = duplicate debits
CORRECT: at-least-once + idempotent consumer = effectively once
```

### What are the edge cases with exactly-once?

True exactly-once is native only to Kafka-to-Kafka flows. When the side effect is an external system like a database, the Kafka transaction cannot include the database write. You achieve effectively-once by making the consumer idempotent.

```java
package com.bank.edge;

public class ExactlyOnceEdgeCases {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Exactly-Once Edge Cases:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Kafka-to-Kafka: true exactly-once");
        System.out.println("  Read from topic A -> enrich -> write to topic B");
        System.out.println("  Transaction covers offset commit + write to B");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Kafka-to-Database: effectively-once");
        System.out.println("  Read from topic A -> write to PostgreSQL");
        System.out.println("  Kafka transaction cannot include DB write");
        System.out.println("  Solution: idempotent consumer (unique constraint)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Kafka-to-External-API: effectively-once");
        System.out.println("  Read from topic A -> call REST API");
        System.out.println("  Solution: idempotency key in API request");
    }
}
```

```text
Kafka-to-Kafka: true exactly-once (transactions)
Kafka-to-Database: effectively-once (idempotent consumer)
Kafka-to-External-API: effectively-once (idempotency key)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Exactly-once has real costs: transactions add latency and throughput overhead. In most banking systems the pragmatic choice is at-least-once plus an idempotent consumer, which gives effectively-once results for database writes without the transactional overhead. Reserve Kafka transactions for genuine Kafka-to-Kafka pipelines.

```java
package com.bank.senior;

public class DeliverySemanticsSeniorGuide {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior Decision Guide:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Metrics/telemetry:  at-most-once (loss OK)");
        System.out.println("Transfers/payments: at-least-once + idempotency");
        System.out.println("Kafka enrichment:   exactly-once (transactions)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Why not exactly-once everywhere?");
        System.out.println("  1. Transaction overhead (latency + throughput)");
        System.out.println("  2. Only covers Kafka-to-Kafka writes");
        System.out.println("  3. External writes still need idempotency");
        System.out.println("  4. Complexity of transactional.id management");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Metrics: at-most-once
Transfers: at-least-once + idempotency (banking default)
Kafka enrichment: exactly-once (transactions)
```
